Erik Hesselink <sel4yixa02DONT@SPAMsneakemail.com> wrote:
> A lot of people dislike your idea, for various reasons. One reason I 
> haven't seen explicitly, is that it removes a decision from the game. 
> Right now, your pet attacks anything it can kill. This means that a 
> powerful pet is a trade-off: you might need a shopkeeper for 
> price-id'ing, a priest for donating, or you might just want to kill 
> monsters to get xp. So before you happily take your powerful pet along, 
> you have to see if you can control it.
> 
> Making your pet not attack peacefuls removes this trade-off. All in all, 
> it removes both some benefits and drawbacks of powerful pets. This, I 
> think, would make the game more boring, less varied, and less fun, which 
> isn't a good thing.

Yes, I agree that powerful pets have disadvantages as well as
advantages.  As I stated elsewhere in this thread, I believe those
include the pet preventing you from getting exp; the pet going
feral/getting confused/ring of conflict; and bones files.  So we have
a total of five disadvantages between the two of us (since we both
list losing exp).  My patch gets rid of the other two disadvantages
you list (price-id'ing and buying protection), but adds in the
disadvantages that neutral and lawful characters can't have their pets
kill shopkeepers and priests without penalty (and thus get a lot of
free money and equipment).  Chaotics don't suffer penalties for murder
(in Vanilla or my version), and so can kill priests/shopkeepers with
or without their pets (although my version requires the chaotic to
aggravate the shopkeeper/priest somehow before the pet will attack). 
So, on the one hand, you have the inability to price-id stuff and buy
protection if you can't control your pets somehow, and on the other,
you have the inability for non-chaotics to wantonly cause (and profit
from) the deaths of "innocents".  Personally, I think that my version
has the stronger disadvantage; if you disagree, I'd be most interested
in knowing why.

I also don't see how my version makes the game more boring, less
varied, or less fun.  If you *want* your pet to attack a peaceful, you
can aggravate it yourself and then step back (if you're fast
enough--better be sure before initiating something!).  From there, the
my version acts like Vanilla, until the pet kills the former
peaceful--at which point neutrals and lawfuls are punished for their
pet's actions (since the player initiated it, and is thus
responsible).  After the combat, the Vanilla benefits/disadvantages
still exist--the player gets all the stuff from the shop, all the
target's money, etc., and loses the abilities to price-id stuff and
buy protection.  *Other than requiring the player to actively initiate
the combat, and holding them responsible for the end result, my
version is exactly the same as Vanilla.*

